intro=[
 "In the spring of 1873 Naser al-Din Shah Qajar, who had ruled Persia for a quarter of a century, left Tehran for a journey no Persian monarch had made: five months by steamer, rail and carriage through Russia, Germany, Belgium, Britain, France, Switzerland, Italy, Austria and the Ottoman Empire, and home through the Caucasus. The trip was the project of his grand vizier, Mirza Hosein Khan Moshir od-Dowleh, who hoped that seeing Europe would win the Shah to railways, ministries and law, and that foreign courts would back the vast concession he had just granted to Baron Julius de Reuter.",
 "The Shah kept a diary every day of the journey, and it was printed in Tehran on his return and translated into English by J. W. Redhouse in 1874. It is a plain, sometimes artless record of distances, meals, palaces, reviews, zoos and theatres, written by a king measuring other kings. It is also curious and frank: about Krupp's steam hammers and the soot of Manchester, the ruins of the Paris Commune, the poverty of southern Italy, and Russian improvements in a Tiflis that Persia had lost.",
 "His hosts wrote back. In London, Queen Victoria's journal, the India Office minder Owen Tudor Burne, the Spectator and Punch, a visiting Mark Twain and a member of the Commons all set down what they saw of him, often on the same days he was writing about them. In Berlin an American diplomat sent home a sharp report on his manners. The record on this side is mostly newspapers, comic weeklies, despatches, parliamentary debate and a later memoir rather than private diaries, and it is labelled that way here.",
 "Both sides' curiosity came mixed with condescension. London papers lectured each other on how to dazzle 'an Oriental', laughed at his table manners and later called his book schoolboyish; Punch made him a Persian cat. The Shah, for his part, read London crowds as personal devotion, judged peoples by their armies and their women, and wrote with the confidence of an absolute ruler. Behind the fireworks lay the Great Game: Britain courted Persia as a buffer against Russia, and Russia watched the courtship.",
 "At home the tour had a cost. Clergy led by Mulla Ali Kani and a court faction around the Shah's wife Anis od-Dowleh, sent home from Moscow, turned against the minister and his concession. When the royal party landed back in Gilan in September, Mirza Hosein Khan was dismissed, and the Reuter concession was cancelled before the year was out. By then a Petersburg columnist could call the whole journey old news.",
]

cal="The Shah's diary is dated by the Islamic lunar (Hijri) calendar, year 1290 AH, and Redhouse prints the Christian (Gregorian) date beside each entry, e.g. 'Tuesday, 24th (22nd April)'. On the evidence of his headers, 1 Rabi' I 1290 fell on 29 April 1873, 1 Rabi' II on 28 May, 1 Jumada I on 27 June, and Jumada II began about 27 July; Rajab dates for late August and September are computed and marked c. Hijri months run 29 or 30 days, so a converted date can be a day off where the header is missing. Russian dates (Dostoevsky's Grazhdanin) are Old Style, twelve days behind the Gregorian calendar in 1873; date_iso always gives the Gregorian date. The Reuter concession of 25 July 1872 fell in Jumada I 1289."

ch=dict(id="naser-1873",title="The King of Kings Goes West: Naser al-Din Shah in Europe",short_title="Naser al-Din Shah in Europe",
 subtitle="The Shah of Persia's first European tour and the hosts who watched him, April–September 1873",
 span="1872–1874",intro=intro,calendar_note=cal,eyes=eyes,entries=entries,claims=claims,convergences=convergences,ties=ties,threads_out=threads,images=images,further=further,
 vantage_key="0 = the Shah and his minister; 1 = hosts and minders at his side; 2 = press and diplomats in the same capital; 3 = Parliament, the concessionaire and the home front at a remove; 4 = a foreign columnist reading the news.")
json.dump(ch,open(OUT,"w",encoding="utf-8"),ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)
print(len(eyes),len(entries),len(claims),len(convergences),len(ties),len(images),len(further))
ids={e["id"] for e in eyes}
for e in entries: assert e["eye"] in ids,e
